1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,681
December 4, 1872.

2
00:00:05,681 --> 00:00:11,361
Four hundred miles east of the Azores, the British cargo ship Dei Gratia
spotted another vessel moving strangely across the North Atlantic.

3
00:00:11,361 --> 00:00:17,042
Her sails were partly set.

4
00:00:17,042 --> 00:00:22,723
Her wheel was unmanned.

5
00:00:22,723 --> 00:00:28,403
No one came on deck to wave back.

6
00:00:28,403 --> 00:00:34,084
Captain David Morehouse ordered a
small boat lowered.

7
00:00:34,084 --> 00:00:39,765
The boarding party climbed up the side and
stepped onto a silent deck.

8
00:00:39,765 --> 00:00:45,446
The wheel turned to nothing.

9
00:00:45,446 --> 00:00:51,126
The ropes were uncoiled but not tangled.

10
00:00:51,126 --> 00:00:56,807
Some sails still drew wind. Others flapped, torn, as if
they had been left without care for days.

11
00:00:56,807 --> 00:01:02,488
Below deck, the picture only
grew stranger.

12
00:01:02,488 --> 00:01:08,168
There were several feet of water in the hold,
but not enough to sink her.

13
00:01:08,168 --> 00:01:13,849
The food and water stores were nearly full.
Enough for weeks at sea.

14
00:01:13,849 --> 00:01:19,530
The cargo — seventeen hundred and one barrels of
industrial alcohol — was almost untouched.

15
00:01:19,530 --> 00:01:25,210
In the captain's cabin, the clothes
were still folded.

16
00:01:25,210 --> 00:01:30,891
The pipes were still on the desk.

17
00:01:30,891 --> 00:01:36,572
The charts were laid out where the
navigator had left them.

18
00:01:36,572 --> 00:01:42,253
The ship's small boat — the yawl — was
missing from its davits.

19
00:01:42,253 --> 00:01:47,933
Where it should have hung, there was empty space and neat tackle. It
had been lowered on purpose. Not torn away by the sea.

20
00:01:47,933 --> 00:01:53,614
The compass was disturbed.

21
00:01:53,614 --> 00:01:59,295
Some of the navigation tools were gone.

22
00:01:59,295 --> 00:02:04,975
In the chart room, the boarding party
found the logbook.

23
00:02:04,975 --> 00:02:10,656
The last clear entry was dated ten days earlier.
After that, the writing simply stopped.

24
00:02:10,656 --> 00:02:16,337
The ship was called the Mary Celeste.

25
00:02:16,337 --> 00:02:22,017
She had ten people aboard when she left New York on
November 7. None of them were on her now.

26
00:02:22,017 --> 00:02:27,698
The story everyone tells about the Mary Celeste
is the story of an empty ship.

27
00:02:27,698 --> 00:02:33,379
The wheel that turned to nothing.

28
00:02:33,379 --> 00:02:39,060
The clothes that were never collected.

29
00:02:39,060 --> 00:02:44,740
The mystery of where the ten people went.

30
00:02:44,740 --> 00:02:50,421
But that story leaves someone out.

31
00:02:50,421 --> 00:02:56,102
The person who lived the longest with what
happened on that ship was not on board.

32
00:02:56,102 --> 00:03:01,782
He was seven years old, two thousand miles away, in a
small house in Marion, Massachusetts.

33
00:03:01,782 --> 00:03:07,463
He was waiting for letters that
would never come.

34
00:03:07,463 --> 00:03:13,144
His name was Arthur Stanley Briggs. He
was seven years old.

35
00:03:13,144 --> 00:03:18,824
He had been left in Massachusetts with his
grandmother — so he could go to school.

36
00:03:18,824 --> 00:03:24,505
His father was the captain of the ship.

37
00:03:24,505 --> 00:03:30,186
Captain Benjamin Spooner Briggs was thirty-seven. He came from a long line of New
England sea captains; almost every man in his family had gone to sea.

38
00:03:30,186 --> 00:03:35,867
He was a steady, fair man, not given to
gambling with his ship or his crew.

39
00:03:35,867 --> 00:03:41,547
He read his Bible, he did not drink, and he had put his own
savings into the Mary Celeste alongside the main owner.

40
00:03:41,547 --> 00:03:47,228
His money, his career, and his reputation
were tied to her wooden hull.

41
00:03:47,228 --> 00:03:52,909
His mother — Arthur's mother — was
Sarah Elizabeth Briggs.

42
00:03:52,909 --> 00:03:58,589
She was thirty years old. The
daughter of a minister.

43
00:03:58,589 --> 00:04:04,270
She had honeymooned with Benjamin on a previous Atlantic crossing, and she was
comfortable at sea in a way few women of her time ever were.

44
00:04:04,270 --> 00:04:09,951
Family letters described her as calm and capable.
The kind of person who did not panic.

45
00:04:09,951 --> 00:04:15,631
Their daughter, Arthur's two-year-old
sister, was named Sophia Matilda.

46
00:04:15,631 --> 00:04:21,312
In late October, in the harbor at Staten Island, Sophia had been
carried aboard the Mary Celeste in her mother's arms.

47
00:04:21,312 --> 00:04:26,993
The ship had been loaded with the alcohol
cargo bound for Genoa, Italy.

48
00:04:26,993 --> 00:04:32,674
There were seven other people on board.

49
00:04:32,674 --> 00:04:38,354
The first mate was Albert Richardson, twenty-eight years old,
an American who had sailed with Briggs before.

50
00:04:38,354 --> 00:04:44,035
The second mate was Andrew Gilling, in his
mid-twenties, born in New York to Danish parents.

51
00:04:44,035 --> 00:04:49,716
The steward and cook was Edward William Head, newly married,
just starting his life ashore between voyages.

52
00:04:49,716 --> 00:04:55,396
Four German sailors from the Frisian Islands made up the rest of the crew — the
brothers Volkert and Boy Lorenzen, Arian Martens, and Gottlieb Goodschaad.

53
00:04:55,396 --> 00:05:01,077
Later research described them as experienced, peaceable men.
First-class sailors. Not known troublemakers.

54
00:05:01,077 --> 00:05:06,758
These were not drifters pulled off the dock at the last minute. They were
men with homes. With families. With reasons to come back.

55
00:05:06,758 --> 00:05:12,438
The ship was delayed by bad weather. While the Mary Celeste waited at anchor near Staten
Island, Sarah used the time to write a letter to her mother-in-law back in Marion.

56
00:05:12,438 --> 00:05:18,119
Tell Arthur, she wrote, that I shall depend on his writing me. Tell him I shall try
to remember things from the voyage that he might enjoy hearing about.

57
00:05:18,119 --> 00:05:23,800
That letter went ashore on November 6. It was the
last anyone in Marion heard from her.

58
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:29,481
The next morning, on November 7, 1872, the
Mary Celeste sailed for Italy.

59
00:05:29,481 --> 00:05:35,161
Arthur, in Massachusetts, went to school.

60
00:05:35,161 --> 00:05:40,842
When the boarding party from the Dei Gratia climbed onto the Mary Celeste
twenty-eight days later, they did not yet know who they were looking for.

61
00:05:40,842 --> 00:05:46,523
They saw an empty wheel. We know now it was the wheel Benjamin Briggs
had stood at every morning of his three-week crossing.

62
00:05:46,523 --> 00:05:52,203
They saw folded clothes in the captain's cabin. We
know now those were Sarah's clothes.

63
00:05:52,203 --> 00:05:57,884
The pipes on the desk were her husband's.

64
00:05:57,884 --> 00:06:03,565
The charts laid out on the table were the ones Benjamin had
been reading the night the writing stopped.

65
00:06:03,565 --> 00:06:09,245
They saw a small toy on the cabin floor.
A child's wooden toy.

66
00:06:09,245 --> 00:06:14,926
They saw a yawl missing from its davits.

67
00:06:14,926 --> 00:06:20,607
We know now that seven men, a captain, a wife, and a two-year-old daughter had climbed into that
small boat — and rowed away from the ship that was their only hope of survival.

68
00:06:20,607 --> 00:06:26,288
The boarding party found a pump taken apart.
They could not understand why.

69
00:06:26,288 --> 00:06:31,968
We know now that the Mary Celeste had been taking on water in her hold, and that Benjamin Briggs — who had
invested his own savings in this ship — had been trying to fix the only tool he had to clear it.

70
00:06:31,968 --> 00:06:37,649
They found the compass disturbed. Some of the navigation instruments missing. We know now
that someone had carried them down into the yawl, expecting to come back.

71
00:06:37,649 --> 00:06:43,330
The Dei Gratia sailed the empty Mary
Celeste to Gibraltar.

72
00:06:43,330 --> 00:06:49,010
The British authorities held a formal inquiry. The attorney
general, Frederick Solly-Flood, suspected foul play.

73
00:06:49,010 --> 00:06:54,691
He suspected murder. He suspected fraud. He
found no evidence of either.

74
00:06:54,691 --> 00:07:00,372
No blood. No bodies. No signs of struggle.

75
00:07:00,372 --> 00:07:06,052
The court ruled that the rescuers deserved a salvage payment. But it
was a small one. The suspicion never quite went away.

76
00:07:06,052 --> 00:07:11,733
While the inquiry continued, the Mary Celeste
sat tied up in Gibraltar harbour.

77
00:07:11,733 --> 00:07:17,414
The clothes were still folded in the cabin.
The toy was still on the floor.

78
00:07:17,414 --> 00:07:23,095
The captain's pipes were still on the desk. The
barrels were still in the hold.

79
00:07:23,095 --> 00:07:28,775
Two thousand miles away in Massachusetts, Arthur
Briggs walked to school every morning.

80
00:07:28,775 --> 00:07:34,456
He had not yet been told that
anything was wrong.

81
00:07:34,456 --> 00:07:40,137
The letters his mother had promised to write — the things she
would try to remember to tell him — never arrived.

82
00:07:40,137 --> 00:07:45,817
The newspapers got there first.

83
00:07:45,817 --> 00:07:51,498
In the months after Gibraltar, the story of the Mary Celeste was
printed and reprinted across the Atlantic world.

84
00:07:51,498 --> 00:07:57,179
The empty ship. The vanished crew. The
unanswered questions.

85
00:07:57,179 --> 00:08:02,859
In 1884, a young writer named Arthur Conan Doyle — not yet famous, not yet the author
of Sherlock Holmes — published a short story about the Mary Celeste.

86
00:08:02,859 --> 00:08:08,540
He changed the names. He invented a murderer. He wrote it as a first-person
confession. Some readers, and at least one newspaper, took it as fact.

87
00:08:08,540 --> 00:08:14,221
The legend grew. The pirates, the mutinies, the
giant squids, the supernatural.

88
00:08:14,221 --> 00:08:19,902
Each version made the ship more haunted. Each version made the people on board less
like real people and more like characters in someone else's story.

89
00:08:19,902 --> 00:08:25,582
Arthur Briggs grew up reading
these stories.

90
00:08:25,582 --> 00:08:31,263
He grew up under the shadow of a mystery
that no one could solve.

91
00:08:31,263 --> 00:08:36,944
The mother who had promised to
write to him.

92
00:08:36,944 --> 00:08:42,624
The father who had invested his savings in a
ship that came home without him.

93
00:08:42,624 --> 00:08:48,305
The little sister who had been carried
aboard in her mother's arms.

94
00:08:48,305 --> 00:08:53,986
He kept the last letter his
mother had sent.

95
00:08:53,986 --> 00:08:59,666
When he was old enough, Arthur Briggs
went to sea himself.

96
00:08:59,666 --> 00:09:05,347
He became the captain of his own ship.

97
00:09:05,347 --> 00:09:11,028
He spent his life on the same Atlantic Ocean
that had taken his family.

98
00:09:11,028 --> 00:09:16,709
In the early 1900s, decades after the Mary Celeste was found drifting, Arthur
Briggs died in a shipwreck off the east coast of the United States.

99
00:09:16,709 --> 00:09:22,389
The ocean took his mother. It took his
father. It took his sister.

100
00:09:22,389 --> 00:09:28,070
And then, when he was old enough to
follow them, it took him too.

